# SAG configuration.
#
# This file is read on the host by docker compose and handed to the container
# as environment variables. Edit it and restart:
#
#   docker compose restart sag
#
# Every variable is documented in docs/configuration.md. Nothing here is
# required to start: with this file empty, SAG runs in development mode on
# http://localhost:8787 and prints sign-in codes to the log.
#
# One warning before you fill it in: this file is committed to the repository
# as comments only, so if you fork SAG, keep your secrets out of it - use your
# platform's secret store, or tell git to stop tracking your copy with
#   git update-index --skip-worktree config.env

# --- What this deployment is ------------------------------------------------
# Set this the moment SAG is reachable at a real name, and put https in front
# of it. Every development default becomes a startup error once it is real.
# SAG_ISSUER=https://id.example.com

# --- Sending the codes ------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465
# EMAIL_FROM=Sign in <no-reply@id.example.com>

# --- Single-use codes and send limits ---------------------------------------
# A container is one process, so the in-memory store is genuinely atomic here.
# Behind more than one container use dynamodb instead: docs/state-and-limits.md
STATE_STORE_BACKEND=memory

# --- Who is signing in ------------------------------------------------------
# UPSTREAM_MICROSOFT_COMMON_CLIENT_ID=common:00000000-1111-2222-3333-444444444444
# UPSTREAM_MICROSOFT_COMMON_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# With more than one of these configured, SAG reads the domain's mail records
# to work out which one holds the account rather than asking. This container
# has a resolver, so the lookup stays inside it. SIGNIN_PROVIDER_HINT=off to
# turn it off, or =order to still ask but put the likely one first.

# --- Who a person is, to a relying party ------------------------------------
# The `sub` in the id_token is derived from the verified address and this salt.
# Required the moment SAG_ISSUER is a real name; until then a well-known
# development salt is used and the start-up banner says so. Set it once and
# never change it: a new salt gives every person a new `sub` everywhere.
# SUBJECT_SALT=                        # openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Every relying party sees the same `sub` by default. SUBJECT_TYPE=pairwise
# gives each one a different value, so two of them cannot work out they are
# talking about the same person.
# SUBJECT_TYPE=pairwise
#
# jamie+shop@example.com is jamie@example.com, because it is one mailbox. Set
# this to false only if tags are how your people keep separate accounts, and
# not after anybody has signed in - it splits every tagged account in two.
# See docs/adr/0011-subject-derived-from-the-verified-address.md.
# SANITISE_PLUS_EMAILS=true

# --- What SAG will say about somebody ---------------------------------------
# On the email code path there is no upstream, so there is no name to relay.
# Guessing one from the address is off by default because `name` is meant to be
# somebody's name; when it is on, the guess is flagged as one. See
# docs/profile-claims.md.
# PROFILE_NAME_FROM_EMAIL=infer
# PROFILE_AVATAR_FALLBACK=initials

# --- What they are signing in to --------------------------------------------
# Relying parties live as JSON files in ./data/clients, one per client id, so
# they can be edited without touching this file. See data/clients/README.md.
CLIENTS_STORE_BACKEND=file
CLIENTS_STORE_DIR=/data/clients

# A handful can go here instead, if you would rather not have files at all:
# CLIENT_LEDGER_ID=ledger
# CLIENT_LEDGER_REDIRECT_URIS=https://ledger.example.com/auth/callback

# --- Appearance -------------------------------------------------------------
# UI_ORG_NAME=Example Ltd
# UI_TERMS_URL=https://example.com/terms
# UI_PRIVACY_URL=https://example.com/privacy

LOG_LEVEL=info
